From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bron Gondwana <brong@fastmailteam.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 13:34:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Add DKIM2 Message-Instance headers at ingress and egress
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Implement the Message-Instance header from draft-ietf-dkim-dkim2-spec-06
so that a DKIM2 verifier can undo the changes a list makes to a message
and re-verify the signatures of earlier hops.

A Message-Instance records SHA-256 hashes of the message headers and
body at one point in the delivery chain (h= and b=), an instance number
(m=), and for m>1 a Recipe: a JSON list of {"c": [start, end]} copy
ranges and {"d": [...]} literal steps that rebuilds the previous
instance's headers and body lines from the current ones.

Ingress handler (message-instance-ingress, first in the posting and
owner pipelines after authenticity validation):

- With no Message-Instance present, add m=1 describing the message as
  received.  The baseline the egress Recipe is computed against is
  msg.original_bytes, which the queue pickles with the message; only the
  two hashes and the instance number go into msgdata['mi_snapshot'].

- With one already present (from an inbound milter, or a signing
  sender), never modify it: it may be covered by a DKIM2-Signature, and
  rewriting or dropping it would destroy the evidence of a broken chain
  and let Mailman pose as the originator.  The baseline is the message
  as received, so everything Mailman did since (the Message-ID-Hash
  stamp, a repaired Message-ID) goes into the egress Recipe; an instance
  that does not match those octets is logged as a warning.  Only without
  received octets (an older queue entry, an injected message) is the
  snapshot picked from the parsed message, using verification as the
  oracle: if it matches the current message, that is the baseline; if
  it only matches once the Message-ID-Hash that Mailman's message store
  stamps on arrival is removed, the unstamped message is the baseline,
  so the egress Recipe records the stamp as a reversible Mailman change.
  A Message-ID-Hash that was part of the signed instance is left alone.

- Digests are skipped; they are not a single authored message.

- On a list with Message-Instance off, the received octets are dropped
  so they are not pickled through every queue.

Egress mixin (MessageInstanceMixin on Deliver and BulkDelivery, after
decoration and ARC signing):

- If the hashes still match the snapshot, add nothing.  Otherwise
  compute header and body Recipes against the snapshot and prepend
  m=N+1.  Hashes and Recipes are over the message as smtplib will send
  it.  When the previous body is one contiguous run of the current one
  (a pure append, or the DKIM2 wrap below) the body Recipe is a single
  copy range found by a linear scan; the line diff, quadratic in
  repeated lines, runs only for other changes.

- A message that already carries an instance newer than the snapshot
  is left alone: BulkDelivery stamps the queued message itself, and a
  temporary failure retries that stamped copy.

- When Mailman rewrote the body itself (content filtering in
  mime_delete, or the DMARC wrap), those handlers set
  msgdata['body-modified'] and the body Recipe is "b": null: the
  previous body cannot be rebuilt, and the Recipe says so rather than
  carrying the whole original as literal data.  A site handler that
  rewrites the body must do the same, or the wrap below would splice in
  the body as received and revert its change.

Decoration on a DKIM2 list wraps instead of re-encoding.  Upstream
re-encodes a single-part text/plain body to append a footer (utf-8
becomes base64) and MIME-wraps anything else after re-serialising it,
so every body line can change.  With Message-Instance enabled for the
list, decorate.py instead splices the received top-level Content-*
fields and body octets in verbatim as the middle part of a new
multipart/mixed, between text/plain header and footer parts, with a
boundary that does not occur in that part and a preamble explaining the
wrap.  The body Recipe is then always literal / one copy range /
literal, whatever the encoding or structure.  Lists without
Message-Instance, messages that did not pass ingress, and bodies
Mailman already rewrote are decorated exactly as upstream does.

- A message with no snapshot and no instance (internally generated via
  the virgin pipeline) gets an originator m=1.

Hashing follows spec-06 §4: the unsigned header list (Received,
Return-Path, Delivered-To, Authentication-Results, DKIM/ARC/DKIM2
signature and Message-Instance headers), plus the X-* and Received-*
prefixes, is excluded; header fields are canonicalised with all
whitespace removed and the body hash strips trailing empty lines.
Header Recipes are omitted rather than emitted as null when only the
body changed.

Header values are folded at tag boundaries to stay within RFC 5322
line length, and the parser unfolds any legal run of folding
whitespace (CRLF plus one or more WSP, spec §2.12 and §2.14), since an
upstream that folds differently must still parse.

Each header we add is accompanied by an X-DKIM2-Info header giving
provenance (draft, repo, date, software) plus the hashed header count
and names, so interop problems can be
diagnosed from the message alone.  It is excluded from the hash by the
X-* rule and is folded to 78 characters.

The feature is off by default; enable it with the [mta]
message_instance option (added with the received octets).

DKIM2-MESSAGE-INSTANCE.md describes the design, the wire format, the
decoration interaction and the resource impact.

Tests cover hashing against interop vectors shared with the other
implementations, Recipe shapes, header folding and unfolding, full
ingress/decorate/egress round trips across 7bit, 8bit,
quoted-printable, base64 and multipart bodies with undo back to m=1,
the DKIM2 wrap, the null body Recipe, the null-recipe guard, and the
milter-then-stamp seam.

Every Message-Instance Mailman adds is accompanied by an X-DKIM2-Info
header in the draft-gondwana-dkim2-debug-header-01 form: a tag-list in
the DKIM2 syntax, every tag followed by ";", the instance action written
"mi-m=<N>". It records the draft implemented, the repository and date,
and the header fields that went into the hash, so interop problems can
be diagnosed from the message alone.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Amended 2026-10-04 after replaying 88 public-archive messages in assorted
charsets through a test list (interop util/charset-corpus.sh):

- Header values are hashed and Recipe'd as the generator emits them
  (policy.fold_binary over raw_items()), not as str(value): str() of the
  Header object subject_prefix stores is the decoded text while the wire
  carries the RFC 2047 form, and a raw 8-bit header is wrapped as
  unknown-8bit by items() but written verbatim by BytesGenerator.
- Recipe header values are unfolded: a Recipe string MUST NOT contain CR
  or LF (spec-06 §5.1).
- Recipe literals whose octets are not ASCII are emitted as {"b": [base64]}
  items (raw octets, 8-bit clean); copy ranges are validated (two integers,
  ascending, non-overlapping, within the item count) and a malformed Recipe
  is never applied at undo.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
